ver time what's known as the  perfect dictatorship the executive power  was predominant over the legislative and  judicial so a fake democratic system  developed based on non-competitive  elections in which the state greatly  reduced civil liberties in doing so the  party controlled most of the economic  and social sectors of the country  denying the possibility of emergence of  social movements ending up dictating the  discipline of illegal activities such as  drug trafficking over the years social  phenomena such as urbanization student  and workers movements internal migration  industrialization and increased levels  of education however have opened the  bridge in the mexican central power  inaugurating in the second half of the  20th century a difficult process of  expansion of political space in addition  a series of reforms of the electoral  system allowed other political forces  such as the conservative right the  national action party and the party of  the democratic revolution full  competition in the municipal state and  national spheres this democratic  transition reached its peak in 2000 with  the presidential victory of vicente fox  quesada at the same time the power  structure changed with a clearer  separation of state powers and greater  decentralization in favor of local and  municipal governments however this  drastic change has created new  opportunities for profit and investment  for narcos access to the free market and  the elimination of fair barriers  especially after the entry into force of  the north american free trade agreement  in 1994 tripled the level of trade with  the united states and opened new ways  for trading illegal goods in addition  the policies that followed eroded the  mexican social fabric greatly reducing  the income of about 50 percent of the  mexican population which fell below the  poverty level pushing millions of people  especially young people to enter the  illegal economy another deep-rooted  problem is the weakness if not in  existence of the state in the internal  areas of the country where successive  governments have always been unable to  deal with the threat of criminal  organizations preferring instead to join  them with a consequence that the most  fragile social categories such as women  children indigenous peoples and migrants  pay the price the increase in the number  of cases of enforced disappearances in  which the state authorities are often  involved contributes to a climate of  total mistrust of the police and justice  agencies which appear unable to provide  definitive answers among the most easily  corruptible subjects are the municipal  authorities who often underpaid by the  federal government are at the mercy of  the criminal power of cartels and  organized gangs for years the country  has been defined with the term narco  state or narco-democracy and this comes  from the combination of narcos political  power and law enforcement a dangerous  link that results in silence impunity  and connivance of those who should  protect mexicans in front of evidence  facts think that in the global ranking  of impunity 2020 mexico occupies the  60th place out of 69 countries analyzed  with the rate of impunity 10 times  higher than the global average let's  take a look at some recent cases in 2018  in acapulco all members of the local  police department were arrested for  suspected ties to organized crime and in  2019 genaro garcia luna federal police  chief and then minister of public  security ended up in jail motivation  turning a blind eye to the movement of  drug shipments in the territory in the  rare cases when investigations are  opened they always go slowly and end up  shedding light in the wrong places but  to understand why drug trafficking finds  such fertile ground in mexico we can  look at a geographical map the country  is strategically located representing an  international hub for illegal substances  natural resources weapons and people  many of them from the south american  continent moreover the particular  confirmation of the territory with  mangroves huge deserts in the north  tropical forests and large mountain  ranges including the eastern and western  sierra madre allows criminal groups to  hide from the government army and  establish areas where they can freely  cultivate marijuana and opium poppy from  which let's remember heroin is processed  otherwise labs are set up for the  production of synthetic drugs such as  fentanyl or methamphetamine both of  which are in great demand in the united  states a country that for years has been  immersed in an epidemic of opium  overdoses but mexico's long coastline  also facilitates under the table  operations and port cities such as  veracruz lazaro cardenas or manzanillo  have become central bastions for many  groups facilitating the entry of drugs  weapons and drug processing chemicals  into their ports it's likely that on  many of the containers that arrive there  is a dragon printed certainly not in  plain sight but with an undeniable  origin yes because businessmen and  traders affiliated to the chinese triads  that send the materials destined to be  transformed into synthetic drugs such as  hefedrine in order to evade controls in  the country of origin have for some time  moved their labs to india and from there  send the products by ship to mexico  where they are skillfully synthesized ok  but how does the united states influence  the mexican criminal situation we have a  fork in the road the first road is the  one of the flourishing u.s market and  its endless demand for narcotics the  second is the one of the prohibitionist  policy that the various us governments  have tried for at least a century to  instill in the leaders of mexico city  over time the so-called war on drugs and  washington's interference in mexican  affairs has shown itself through  unilateral actions of border  interdiction such as operation intercept  of 1969 and in the financing of aerial  fumigation campaigns of opium and  marijuana crops sea operation condor of  1975. the attention of the white house  towards mexico was also expressed by  planning undercover operations of u.s  federal agents in particular the dea  since the 90s however security  cooperation has grown culminating in  2008 with the entry into force of the  initiative amerida the most serious  attempt to put on paper at a bilateral  level the fight against the drug cartels  however if we are talking about  trafficking we must mention another  scourge that of the illegal traffic of  migrants according to the most reliable  estimates every year at least the 2  million people including children  traveling alone in search of fortune  cross or try to cross the border into  the united states that's why as the los  angeles times reports nearly 25 percent  of the 45 million immigrants in north  america are of mexican origin also in  this case the activity is under the  control of criminal organizations in  complicity with mexican law enforcement  agencies over the years there have been  cases where migrants were arrested by  police officers and then sold to various  criminal groups with the goal of  obtaining ransoms from their families  and i leave you to imagine the end of  those who couldn't afford such sums the  numbers speak of 84 000 desapresidos two  years ago some of whom disappeared in  acid with all this data it won't sound  strange to you to hear that mexico is  one of the most violent countries in the  world according to mexico's national  institute of statistics and geography as  of 2018 the country has maintained a  rate of 29 murders per 100 000  inhabitants a rate higher even than  countries that have historically had a  higher rate such as el salvador with its  20 homicides per 100 000 inhabitants in  2020 or colombia with a rate of less  than 24 to give you just an idea of the  magnitude even though one shouldn't  compare countries with such distant and  different geographical contexts in italy  in 2019 the homicide rate was 0.53 100  000 inhabitants however among the most  affected areas are the states of  michoacan tamaulipas and zacatecas where  clashes between gangs are almost the  order of the day often these clashes  also end up involving civilians as  happened during the massacre of  ayotsinapa with the disappearance of a  bus carrying 43 students to the city of  iguala the boys were stopped by the  police arrested on charges of wanting to  join a protest and finally handed over  to the guerrero sunidos clan who killed  them and made the bodies disappear their  murder can be explained if we consider  that the students of escuela normale  rural are people of humble origins and  actively interested in the  socio-political problems that afflict  their land to the point that in recent  years hundreds of social fighters have  emerged from those classrooms a  criminalization unfortunately remained  unpunished due to the obstructionism of  many politicians local authorities and  journalists who spread false information  about the incident preventing the truth  from coming out or think of the massacre  of san fernando by the cartel de los  zetas in the summer of 2010 where 72 of  the 73 migrants bound for the united  states were kidnapped and brutally  killed the same fate then happened to  the judge in charge of the investigation  and you may ask has justice been done no  because it's worth repeating federal  institutions and drug traffickers go  hand in hand talking about violence in  mexico is also the same as talking about  gender violence the data at our disposal  speak for themselves according to a  government report in 2020 two-thirds of  mexican women aged 15 years and older  have experienced violence at least once  in their lives and also in the same year  feminiside has reached worrying heights  with a record of over 3 000 women killed  all this happens with almost complete  silence of the judicial authorities  evidence disappeared forensic analysis  that remain pending abandoned lines of  investigation make it seriously  difficult to fight the problem of  violence against women but now in order  to better understand this world  situation of chaos we should talk about  the real lords of the country the  drunkard tells even an uninformed person  knows that in the 90s the throne of the  king of cocaine was occupied by the  infamous pablo escobar who together with  the rival cartel of cali dominated the  international traffic of cocaine but in  the last two decades the supremacy of  mexican organizations in drug  trafficking has replaced the colombian  one becoming the main regional threat  and where do they practice their dirty  operations like smuggling tons of drugs  and thousands of illegal migrants of  course the us border and they do it  using extreme and unconditional violence  as an instrument of terror and control  of society however the border is not  accidental because the big cartels are  well aware that the dominance of this  area guarantees profits of millions of  dollars with which they constantly  self-finance their activities and how  were these realities born in 2006 in  mexico there were seven cartels  millennial sinaloa juarez tijuana the  golf cartel la familia michoacana and  the pacific cartel their birth was  facilitated by the proximity of the  united states the main global economy as  well as the largest consumer of drugs  simple supply and demand although the  production and consumption of substances  in mexico especially those derived from  the opium poppy and marijuana date back  to the end of the 19th century it was  after world war ii that things began to  take a warring turn in the 1960s drugs  such as those mentioned above began to  be produced in the states of sinaloa  durango chihuahua michoacan and guerrero  regions from which particularly sinaloa  it was easy to creep into the united  states one of the fundamental changes  then occurred in the 1980s when colombia  narcos shifted their coca roots from the  caribbean to mexico helping the  emergence of one of the first two major  organizations the guadalajara cardel led  by miguel gallardo nicknamed the  godfather the boss of bosses gallardo  was originally an agent of the mexican  federal judicial police el jefe quickly  became not only the first mexican to act  as a link with the colombian cartels in  their heyday but also the first to  become a point of reference for the  medellin cartel this link allowed him to  request a new form of payment if  gallardo was paid in cash for the  transport of cocaine from the mexican  border to the united states from a  certain moment onwards he began to  withhold 50 percent of the transported  product a fact that allowed the narcos  of the guadalajara cartel to transform  themselves from transporters into  distributors thereby greatly increasing  their profits although it was not the  only organization present this cartel  managed to gain a monopoly of the major  marijuana and opium producing areas as  well as the main access points to the us  border such as the states of baja  california and sonora and how could he  act undisturbed simple in addition to  his affiliates politicians and entire  police departments protected him  remember in 1985 gallardo's thugs  managed to kidnap and kill enrique  camarena a dea agent who was  investigating the organization's  trafficking the incident provoked the  more active involvement of the us agents  that ended with the arrest of the main  leaders of the cartel and the  fragmentation of the same with  guadalajara out of the way in the 1980s  three other large monopolistic groups  remained the tijuana juarez and sinaloa  cartels the competition between them  triggered the violence conflict from  which the latter emerged the winner  gaining control of the main routes  without forgetting its more  international dimension compared to its  competitors its overwhelming power  derived mainly 